# DKMS installation

DKMS manages only `crystalhd.ko`. Install firmware, the udev rule and native
userspace separately with `sudo make install-runtime`. The DKMS configuration
does not run a post-install firmware hook.

Install DKMS and headers for the running kernel:

```sh
sudo apt install dkms linux-headers-$(uname -r)
```

## First registration

From the repository root, first check whether this version already has a
registered source directory:

```sh
dkms status -m crystalhd
readlink -f /usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0 2>/dev/null || true
```

Only when `/usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0` does not exist, register this checkout:

```sh
sudo ln -s "$PWD" /usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0
sudo dkms add -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0
```

Do not overwrite an existing link blindly. If it resolves to another checkout,
either keep using that tree or deliberately remove its DKMS registration before
registering this one.

Build and install for the selected kernel, then install the non-module runtime:

```sh
kernel_release=$(uname -r)
sudo dkms build -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$kernel_release"
sudo dkms install -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$kernel_release"
make -j"$(nproc)" library gstreamer vaapi
sudo make install-runtime
```

If a direct install previously created
`/lib/modules/$kernel_release/updates/crystalhd.ko`, it can shadow the DKMS
copy. Remove only that configured direct file with:

```sh
sudo make uninstall-module KVER="$kernel_release"
```

This does not unload the running module or remove
`updates/dkms/crystalhd.ko*`.

## Updating the same DKMS version

When the registered `/usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0` link already resolves to this
checkout, rebuild the selected kernel explicitly:

```sh
kernel_release=$(uname -r)
sudo dkms build --force -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$kernel_release"
sudo dkms install --force -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 -k "$kernel_release"
make -j"$(nproc)" library gstreamer vaapi
sudo make install-runtime
crystalhd-check --source-tree "$PWD"
```

`--force` is appropriate here only because replacing the same registered
version is intentional. The preflight lists all direct and DKMS candidates,
the module selected for the next load and the module already in memory.

If the loaded source version is stale, close all clients and confirm that the
device is idle before a normal reload:

```sh
sudo fuser -v /dev/crystalhd
sudo modprobe -r crystalhd
sudo modprobe crystalhd
crystalhd-check
```

Proceed only when `fuser` shows no owner. Never force-unload an active module;
reboot instead if a normal unload reports that the module is busy.

## Removal

Remove the DKMS-owned modules independently from the runtime files:

```sh
sudo dkms remove -m crystalhd -v 3.10.0 --all
sudo make uninstall-runtime
```

The first command does not unload a module already in memory. The second
removes firmware, the udev rule, library, headers and frontends; it does not
touch DKMS or browser profiles.

Remove `/usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0` only after confirming that it is the link
you created for this checkout:

```sh
registered_source=$(readlink -f /usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0)
checkout_source=$(pwd -P)
if [ "$registered_source" = "$checkout_source" ]; then
  sudo rm -- /usr/src/crystalhd-3.10.0
else
  echo "Refusing to remove a different registered source" >&2
fi
```

If the equality check fails, stop and inspect the existing registration rather
than deleting another source tree's link.
